<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kingdom of Zimbabwe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> was the state centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwe plateau</a>, which flourished from about the twelfth to the fifteenth century. Its stone enclosures and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">gold trade</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coast made it a major southern African power. Its decline was followed by the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire</a>, which inherited much of its political and commercial tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-zimbabwe.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Zimbabwe" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cefala_Manich_Simbaoe.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The kingdom was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a> state named after its capital; dzimbabwe is Shona for great house of stone. The site had been occupied since about 1000 by speakers of proto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karanga">Karanga</a> and lay on the fringe of the Mapungubwe state. From the 12th century it competed with settlements such as Chivowa, and its ruling dynasty built houses of thick daga and stone walls to shield themselves from view. By 1250 it was an important trading centre, and by 1300 merchants were bypassing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> for the gold-producing interior, which brought the rapid decline of Mapungubwe and the dominance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At its height the capital covered 7.22 square kilometres and had about 10,000 people, and the state, made up of over 150 smaller zimbabwes, likely covered 50,000 square kilometres. It did not control the village mines directly but dominated the trade routes, exporting gold through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a>.</p><p>The causes of its decline are unknown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a> tradition speaks of a shortage of salt, perhaps a figure for exhausted land or resources, and from the early 15th century falling demand for gold hurt the city. Tradition says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyatsimba-mutota">Nyatsimba Mutota</a> led people north to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire</a>, and by the 16th century power had passed to Mutapa and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khami">Khami</a>, capital of the Kingdom of Butua. The site was likely inhabited into the 17th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The sacred king, the mambo, was helped by a chosen brother or sister and ruled over a three-tiered class structure, taxing tributary rulers. Royalty lived first in the Eastern and Western enclosures and later in the Great Enclosure, while district chiefs and regional governors had prestige enclosures outside. Wealth rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, farming and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">gold trade</a>, and Persian bowls and Chinese celadon reached the city.</p><p>From the 1890s to the 1920s European antiquarians looted the site, and Rhodesians, out of racial prejudice, credited it to anyone but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a>; its African origin became the consensus only by the 1950s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kingdom of Great Zimbabwe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to tradition, it was founded around 1430 by Nyatsimba Mutota, a prince associated with the declining <b>Kingdom of Zimbabwe</b>, centred on Great Zimbabwe, who moved northward in search of new sources of salt and fertile land.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
